## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of tokenization-to-attention flow in a real task like headline classification. It connects a toy numeric example to concepts used in production.

## Key Concepts
- Self-attention with Q, K, V
- Scaling by sqrt(dk) and softmax
- How token representations influence weights and output

## Code Example
```javascript
function attention(q, K, V) {
  // q: 1xd, K: nxd, V: nxd
  const dk = q.length;
  const scores = K.map(k => dot(q, k) / Math.sqrt(dk));
  const weights = softmax(scores);
  const out = [0,0].map((_,i) => weights.reduce((acc, w, idx) => acc + w * V[idx][i], 0));
  return {weights, out};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multi-head attention and what changes in compute cost?
- How do embedding size and padding affect numerical stability and softmax behavior?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical RAG design for payments, emphasizing chunking, embedding sizing, vector DB indexing, latency, and freshness controls in real-world contexts.

## Key Concepts

- Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)
- Document chunking and overlap strategies
- Vector databases (e.g., Pinecone, Weaviate)
- Re-ranking and freshness management

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: chunk 4KB with 25% overlap
const CHUNK_SIZE = 4 * 1024;
const OVERLAP = Math.floor(CHUNK_SIZE * 0.25);
function chunk(doc) {
  const blocks = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < doc.length; i += CHUNK_SIZE - OVERLAP) {
    blocks.push(doc.slice(i, i + CHUNK_SIZE));
  }
  return blocks;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor vector DB drift and trigger reindexing automatically?
- How would you evaluate retrieval quality in production (precision@K, user feedback)?
